WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.355 --> 00:00:01.800
(light music)

00:00:01.800 --> 00:00:02.800
- [Announcer] This Great Big Story

00:00:02.800 --> 00:00:06.798
is made possible by
Lexus, experience amazing.

00:00:06.798 --> 00:00:09.298
(light music)

00:00:17.423 --> 00:00:19.490
- The Harvard Brain Bank is one of

00:00:19.490 --> 00:00:22.160
the largest brain banks in the country.

00:00:22.160 --> 00:00:27.111
At the moment we house
approximately 5,000 brain specimens.

00:00:27.111 --> 00:00:29.611
(light music)

00:00:32.687 --> 00:00:35.940
My name is Sabina Berretta,
I am the Scientific Director

00:00:35.940 --> 00:00:38.170
of the Harvard Brain
Tissue Resource Center,

00:00:38.170 --> 00:00:41.800
which we affectionately call
the "Harvard Brain Bank."

00:00:41.800 --> 00:00:44.560
Our mission is to collect brain specimens,

00:00:44.560 --> 00:00:48.240
store them, characterize them,
and then redistribute them

00:00:48.240 --> 00:00:50.813
out to investigators across the world.

00:00:52.122 --> 00:00:54.370
They do research on the human brain,

00:00:54.370 --> 00:00:56.580
and in particular on large number

00:00:56.580 --> 00:00:59.400
of neurological and
psychiatric brain disorders

00:00:59.400 --> 00:01:02.580
such as schizophrenia, bipolar disorder,

00:01:02.580 --> 00:01:05.163
Parkinson, Huntington, and many others.

00:01:06.920 --> 00:01:08.620
The Brain Bank never sleeps.

00:01:08.620 --> 00:01:13.600
We have two sets of people
that are on call 24/7.

00:01:13.600 --> 00:01:15.850
We have a very limited time window

00:01:15.850 --> 00:01:18.330
from when the person passes away

00:01:18.330 --> 00:01:21.603
to the time we need to be
done with this whole process.

00:01:22.490 --> 00:01:25.190
- So this is gonna be your first
brain late at night, right?

00:01:25.190 --> 00:01:27.100
- I know, first late-night brain.

00:01:27.100 --> 00:01:30.670
- We have to do it in less than 24 hours.

00:01:30.670 --> 00:01:33.230
Our courier will bring
it to McLean Hospital

00:01:33.230 --> 00:01:35.127
to the Brain Bank.

00:01:35.127 --> 00:01:36.040
- [Male Dissectionist]
So just put it out here,

00:01:36.040 --> 00:01:37.690
so we're gonna take it ourselves.

00:01:44.384 --> 00:01:48.028
- There will be a dissectionist
ready to dissect the brain.

00:01:48.028 --> 00:01:51.150
(light music)

00:01:51.150 --> 00:01:54.280
That involves separating
the two hemispheres.

00:01:54.280 --> 00:01:56.200
One of the two hemispheres is put

00:01:56.200 --> 00:01:58.510
in a solution that preserves it.

00:01:58.510 --> 00:02:03.240
The other hemisphere will be
dissected in thick sections

00:02:03.240 --> 00:02:04.953
and also will be frozen.

00:02:06.600 --> 00:02:09.560
We are not even close to understanding

00:02:09.560 --> 00:02:12.700
a lot of what we take for granted,

00:02:12.700 --> 00:02:17.700
our thoughts, our instincts,
our feelings, our emotions.

00:02:17.800 --> 00:02:19.827
There are a lot of questions
we still don't know,

00:02:19.827 --> 00:02:22.770
and a lot of our brain function still

00:02:22.770 --> 00:02:26.440
will probably be studied
for a long time to come.

00:02:26.440 --> 00:02:29.500
A brain donation is,
in a very concrete way,

00:02:29.500 --> 00:02:32.600
a gift of knowledge and it cannot be done

00:02:32.600 --> 00:02:34.930
without the help of families

00:02:34.930 --> 00:02:37.677
and people willing to donate their brain.

00:02:37.677 --> 00:02:40.177
(light music)

00:02:42.821 --> 00:02:45.321
(light music)

00:02:56.044 --> 00:02:57.634
- Give me a hug, give me a hug.

00:02:57.634 --> 00:03:00.053
Yes, I know, good girl.

00:03:00.053 --> 00:03:00.920
Wanna do some training?

00:03:00.920 --> 00:03:04.420
I work with dogs to train
them to into an MRI scanner

00:03:04.420 --> 00:03:06.960
and try to figure out what makes them tick

00:03:06.960 --> 00:03:08.290
and what they're thinking.

00:03:08.290 --> 00:03:10.790
(light music)

00:03:15.875 --> 00:03:18.375
(light music)

00:03:19.470 --> 00:03:21.830
My name is Gregory Berns
I'm a neuroscientist

00:03:21.830 --> 00:03:23.903
at Emory University in Atlanta, Georgia.

00:03:25.900 --> 00:03:28.450
I've grown up with dogs,
and I've lived with dogs

00:03:28.450 --> 00:03:29.750
pretty much my whole life.

00:03:31.210 --> 00:03:33.057
One of my favorite dogs had passed away

00:03:33.057 --> 00:03:35.780
and in the back of my
mind I started wondering,

00:03:35.780 --> 00:03:39.040
did that dog love me in
the way that I loved him,

00:03:39.040 --> 00:03:41.180
and that was really the beginning of it.

00:03:41.180 --> 00:03:43.720
All of these basic things that we've begun

00:03:43.720 --> 00:03:46.520
to sort out in humans,
at least in human brains,

00:03:46.520 --> 00:03:47.900
we want to do in dogs.

00:03:47.900 --> 00:03:50.960
If dogs can be trained to
go into an MRI machine,

00:03:50.960 --> 00:03:52.180
then maybe we could figure out

00:03:52.180 --> 00:03:53.960
what they're actually thinking.

00:03:53.960 --> 00:03:56.710
So we have a dog in the
scanner, that's Katie,

00:03:56.710 --> 00:03:59.690
and she is a veteran of the project,

00:03:59.690 --> 00:04:03.095
this is probably about the
15th time she's done this.

00:04:03.095 --> 00:04:05.595
(light music)

00:04:08.060 --> 00:04:10.000
Just having the idea of,

00:04:10.000 --> 00:04:12.470
okay, well let's train
dogs to go in the scanner,

00:04:12.470 --> 00:04:15.150
it wasn't immediately apparent how

00:04:15.150 --> 00:04:16.420
you would go about doing that,

00:04:16.420 --> 00:04:21.127
and MRI scanners are not the
most pleasant environments.

00:04:21.127 --> 00:04:23.410
(MRI whirring)

00:04:23.410 --> 00:04:25.157
Okay, hey, Callie.

00:04:25.157 --> 00:04:26.900
Cool, good girl.

00:04:26.900 --> 00:04:29.160
Callie is the little black terrier

00:04:29.160 --> 00:04:32.500
who was the first dog to
train for the dog project.

00:04:32.500 --> 00:04:33.333
High-five.

00:04:33.333 --> 00:04:35.720
I built a simulator of an MRI.

00:04:35.720 --> 00:04:39.730
So, this is the simulator that
I built here in my basement,

00:04:39.730 --> 00:04:42.470
then we started adding in
recordings of the scanner noise,

00:04:42.470 --> 00:04:44.177
which is actually quite loud.

00:04:44.177 --> 00:04:45.130
(MRI whirring)

00:04:45.130 --> 00:04:47.710
We just started working with treats

00:04:47.710 --> 00:04:49.400
and positive reinforcement to see

00:04:49.400 --> 00:04:51.500
if we could get her to go into this thing.

00:04:52.670 --> 00:04:55.000
That actually proved to be pretty easy.

00:04:55.000 --> 00:04:56.950
And then so what we did
was we kind of put out

00:04:56.950 --> 00:04:58.820
word of mouth, do you
wanna join this project,

00:04:58.820 --> 00:05:00.640
do you wanna train your dogs for an MRI,

00:05:00.640 --> 00:05:02.440
and maybe figure out
what they're thinking.

00:05:02.440 --> 00:05:03.780
The purpose of this is to go through

00:05:03.780 --> 00:05:06.520
a number of exercises
including basic obedience,

00:05:06.520 --> 00:05:09.333
as well as how they
react to the obstacles.

00:05:10.190 --> 00:05:13.980
When we hold the try-outs
it lets us weed out the dogs

00:05:13.980 --> 00:05:16.980
that we don't think
would enjoy doing this.

00:05:16.980 --> 00:05:20.230
One of the benefits then
of having more participants

00:05:20.230 --> 00:05:21.890
is it gives you statistical power.

00:05:21.890 --> 00:05:24.550
You can start to average
dog's brains together

00:05:24.550 --> 00:05:26.980
and get a better sense
for what's happening.

00:05:26.980 --> 00:05:28.640
I started wondering very seriously

00:05:28.640 --> 00:05:31.040
if we could really finally
answer this question,

00:05:31.040 --> 00:05:35.460
do dogs essentially like us
just for the social bond,

00:05:35.460 --> 00:05:36.961
not about the food.

00:05:36.961 --> 00:05:38.170
- [Man] Good boy.

00:05:38.170 --> 00:05:41.140
- And what we found was,
looking at the reward system,

00:05:41.140 --> 00:05:43.880
that almost all the dogs
had equal responses,

00:05:43.880 --> 00:05:46.340
both to food as well as praise,

00:05:46.340 --> 00:05:48.893
and even a few dogs liked the praise more.

00:05:51.140 --> 00:05:53.540
Things that we were finding
about the dog's brain

00:05:53.540 --> 00:05:55.580
in many ways confirmed, I think,

00:05:55.580 --> 00:05:58.210
what people know in their hearts about

00:05:58.210 --> 00:06:00.463
how dogs behave and why they behave.

00:06:02.647 --> 00:06:04.410
And the way I think about
dogs is, in many ways,

00:06:04.410 --> 00:06:07.332
they're the ambassadors
to the animal world.

00:06:07.332 --> 00:06:08.180
(barking)

00:06:08.180 --> 00:06:09.780
They're not that different from

00:06:09.780 --> 00:06:11.190
many of the other mammals out there,

00:06:11.190 --> 00:06:14.260
and so I suspect that a
lot of what we find in dogs

00:06:14.260 --> 00:06:17.339
probably holds true for
pretty much any mammal.

00:06:17.339 --> 00:06:19.839
(light music)

00:06:21.770 --> 00:06:24.270
(light music)

00:06:31.929 --> 00:06:34.710
(light music)

00:06:34.710 --> 00:06:36.250
- [Narrator] Have you ever
walked out of a building

00:06:36.250 --> 00:06:39.573
on a sunny day and had an
uncontrollable urge to sneeze?

00:06:41.270 --> 00:06:44.030
If you answered yes, like
me, you likely suffer

00:06:44.030 --> 00:06:46.430
from a strange genetic
condition that has remained

00:06:46.430 --> 00:06:49.083
unexplained by medical
science for millennia.

00:06:50.690 --> 00:06:52.040
And if you answered no, you probably have

00:06:52.040 --> 00:06:53.863
no idea what I'm talking about.

00:06:55.810 --> 00:06:58.220
Sun sneezing, as it's more commonly known,

00:06:58.220 --> 00:07:01.800
is a medical condition called
the Photic Sneeze Reflex.

00:07:01.800 --> 00:07:03.990
- [Dr. Louis] Another name
that some have given is--

00:07:03.990 --> 00:07:06.780
- [Narrator] Hold on,
before we get into all that,

00:07:06.780 --> 00:07:10.860
this is Dr. Louis Ptacek, a
neurologist and human geneticist

00:07:10.860 --> 00:07:13.830
at the University of
California, San Francisco.

00:07:13.830 --> 00:07:14.670
Okay, go ahead.

00:07:14.670 --> 00:07:17.070
- [Dr. Louis] Another name
that some have given is

00:07:17.070 --> 00:07:21.300
autosomal dominant compelling
helio-ophthalmic outburst,

00:07:21.300 --> 00:07:24.570
or ACHOO Syndrome, but P.S.R. is really

00:07:24.570 --> 00:07:26.560
the appropriate name
that we should all use.

00:07:26.560 --> 00:07:29.610
Photic Sneeze Reflex
is a tendency to sneeze

00:07:29.610 --> 00:07:31.970
when suddenly exposed to bright light.

00:07:31.970 --> 00:07:33.700
(sneezing)

00:07:33.700 --> 00:07:35.240
- [Narrator] And as
strange as that may sound

00:07:35.240 --> 00:07:37.150
it's more common than you might think.

00:07:37.150 --> 00:07:40.020
- [Dr. Louis] I estimate
that about one in 10, or 10%,

00:07:40.020 --> 00:07:42.243
of all people have this condition.

00:07:43.637 --> 00:07:44.470
(sneezing)

00:07:44.470 --> 00:07:45.720
- [Narrator] So a lot of us are affected,

00:07:45.720 --> 00:07:49.150
but how and why it happens
is not fully understood,

00:07:49.150 --> 00:07:50.760
despite the fact that humans have been

00:07:50.760 --> 00:07:54.460
pondering it for thousands of years.

00:07:54.460 --> 00:07:57.657
In 350 B.C. Aristotle posed the question,

00:07:57.657 --> 00:08:00.510
"Why does the heat of the
sun provoke sneezing?"

00:08:00.510 --> 00:08:03.610
Aristotle was barking up
the wrong tree though.

00:08:03.610 --> 00:08:05.073
- [Dr. Louis] This doesn't makes sense

00:08:05.073 --> 00:08:09.670
because heat in and of itself
doesn't cause sneezing.

00:08:09.670 --> 00:08:11.730
- [Narrator] Some 2,000
years later the philosopher,

00:08:11.730 --> 00:08:14.270
Sir Francis Bacon, also
tried to figure it out.

00:08:14.270 --> 00:08:16.160
His theory was that light from the sun

00:08:16.160 --> 00:08:17.700
caused your eyes to water,

00:08:17.700 --> 00:08:20.851
which then seeped into your
nose triggering a sneeze.

00:08:20.851 --> 00:08:21.684
(sneezing)

00:08:21.684 --> 00:08:22.550
- [Dr. Louis] It's an interesting thought

00:08:22.550 --> 00:08:24.360
but it doesn't make a lot of sense.

00:08:24.360 --> 00:08:26.750
P.S.R. occurs almost instantaneously

00:08:26.750 --> 00:08:30.250
and it takes longer for
the tear ducts to react.

00:08:30.250 --> 00:08:34.150
Furthermore, tears don't cause sneezing.

00:08:34.150 --> 00:08:36.300
There's no evidence for that.

00:08:36.300 --> 00:08:37.220
- [Narrator] So modern medicine

00:08:37.220 --> 00:08:39.310
has come with its own theories,

00:08:39.310 --> 00:08:42.200
and it has to do with
the wiring in your head.

00:08:42.200 --> 00:08:43.960
- [Dr. Louis] Sneezes
are triggered typically

00:08:43.960 --> 00:08:47.250
by irritating things in our nose,

00:08:47.250 --> 00:08:50.310
and these are detected by
nerve, the trigeminal nerve.

00:08:50.310 --> 00:08:52.120
- [Narrator] And right next
door to the trigeminal nerve

00:08:52.120 --> 00:08:54.570
is the optic nerve, you
may have heard of this one.

00:08:54.570 --> 00:08:56.970
- [Dr. Louis] The optic nerve
is stimulated by bright light

00:08:56.970 --> 00:08:59.430
and sends electric signals to the brain.

00:08:59.430 --> 00:09:02.950
Some of that signal can be
picked up by the trigeminal nerve

00:09:02.950 --> 00:09:04.790
and misinterpreted by the brain

00:09:04.790 --> 00:09:06.440
as an irritation in the nose.

00:09:06.440 --> 00:09:09.720
- [Narrator] Which triggers,
you guessed it, a sneeze.

00:09:09.720 --> 00:09:11.910
Well, that's the theory anyway.

00:09:11.910 --> 00:09:14.530
- [Dr. Louis] Photic Sneeze
Reflex is not a disease,

00:09:14.530 --> 00:09:16.460
consequently the medical community

00:09:16.460 --> 00:09:19.010
hasn't paid a whole
lot of attention to it,

00:09:19.010 --> 00:09:21.900
therefore we know very
little about what causes it.

00:09:21.900 --> 00:09:23.270
- [Narrator] But Dr. Ptacek believes

00:09:23.270 --> 00:09:25.470
that understanding
P.S.R. could potentially

00:09:25.470 --> 00:09:28.470
shed some light on more
serious conditions.

00:09:28.470 --> 00:09:29.490
- [Dr. Louis] Epileptic seizures

00:09:29.490 --> 00:09:31.300
can be caused by strobing lights,

00:09:31.300 --> 00:09:34.420
migraine headache is
precipitated by light.

00:09:34.420 --> 00:09:37.260
If we can understand what the mechanism is

00:09:37.260 --> 00:09:39.760
that leads to P.S.R.,
this may give insights

00:09:39.760 --> 00:09:41.450
into other conditions which actually

00:09:41.450 --> 00:09:46.450
are medically important
and represent disorders.

00:09:46.470 --> 00:09:47.330
- [Narrator] So, there you have it.

00:09:47.330 --> 00:09:49.250
This 2,000-year-old medical mystery

00:09:49.250 --> 00:09:50.993
may be worth solving after all.

00:09:52.974 --> 00:09:55.891
(orchestral music)

00:10:00.060 --> 00:10:01.380
- [Narrator] Imagine remembering

00:10:01.380 --> 00:10:05.123
every single day of your
life, all in perfect detail.

00:10:05.960 --> 00:10:09.180
For those with Highly Superior
Autobiographical Memory,

00:10:09.180 --> 00:10:12.733
or H.S.A.M. for short,
this is their reality.

00:10:13.605 --> 00:10:16.105
(light music)

00:10:19.228 --> 00:10:21.660
- [Rebecca] I remember
everything that's personally

00:10:21.660 --> 00:10:25.260
happened to me since I was born.

00:10:25.260 --> 00:10:26.880
- [Narrator] That's Rebecca Sharuk.

00:10:26.880 --> 00:10:28.640
I was fascinated by this condition,

00:10:28.640 --> 00:10:31.083
so I called her up to find out more.

00:10:31.083 --> 00:10:33.520
(phone ringing)

00:10:33.520 --> 00:10:34.360
Hi, Rebecca.

00:10:34.360 --> 00:10:35.380
- [Rebecca] Hi.

00:10:35.380 --> 00:10:36.310
- [Narrator] How are you doing?

00:10:36.310 --> 00:10:37.840
- [Rebecca] I'm doing all right.

00:10:37.840 --> 00:10:41.230
It's a very wet Saturday morning here.

00:10:41.230 --> 00:10:42.800
How are you doing?

00:10:42.800 --> 00:10:45.340
- [Narrator] Side note, in
this moment I just realized

00:10:45.340 --> 00:10:46.880
Rebecca will remember that this day

00:10:46.880 --> 00:10:48.593
was a wet morning forever.

00:10:50.760 --> 00:10:53.860
So, you remember
everything in great detail.

00:10:53.860 --> 00:10:54.860
- [Rebecca] People can remember

00:10:54.860 --> 00:10:57.260
what they did last Saturday,

00:10:57.260 --> 00:11:02.260
but I can remember what I
did Saturday 10 years ago.

00:11:02.950 --> 00:11:03.830
- [Narrator] What about a memory

00:11:03.830 --> 00:11:05.880
from when you were just a baby?

00:11:05.880 --> 00:11:08.880
- [Rebecca] When I was twelve days old

00:11:08.880 --> 00:11:13.420
I was lying down on the fur
car seat in my parents' car,

00:11:13.420 --> 00:11:15.970
and I was staring up
at the steering wheel,

00:11:15.970 --> 00:11:18.610
and I thought, "What is that?"

00:11:18.610 --> 00:11:21.380
- [Narrator] Rebecca is one
of only 60 people world-wide

00:11:21.380 --> 00:11:23.780
to have been identified
with having H.S.A.M.

00:11:23.780 --> 00:11:26.100
- [Rebecca] All through my teenage years

00:11:26.100 --> 00:11:29.840
I thought that everybody
remembered in this kind of way,

00:11:29.840 --> 00:11:31.910
I thought it was completely normal.

00:11:31.910 --> 00:11:33.800
- [Narrator] It wasn't until she was 21

00:11:33.800 --> 00:11:36.080
when her parents decided to submit her

00:11:36.080 --> 00:11:37.560
to the University of California

00:11:37.560 --> 00:11:39.660
to undergo two years of testing.

00:11:39.660 --> 00:11:41.900
After this, she was officially identified

00:11:41.900 --> 00:11:43.063
as having H.S.A.M.

00:11:45.060 --> 00:11:46.760
Since memories can come to mind

00:11:46.760 --> 00:11:50.380
unexpectedly any time of day,
a negative memory like this

00:11:50.380 --> 00:11:52.860
can make living with H.S.A.M. difficult.

00:11:52.860 --> 00:11:56.970
- [Rebecca] Mum was putting
me in this itchy, satin dress

00:11:56.970 --> 00:11:59.230
and I was crying because I didn't like it,

00:11:59.230 --> 00:12:02.160
and Mum gave me this Minnie Mouse toy,

00:12:02.160 --> 00:12:04.590
and I couldn't say anything at the time,

00:12:04.590 --> 00:12:07.300
but her face terrified me.

00:12:07.300 --> 00:12:09.520
- [Narrator] But on most
days, H.S.A.M. is a gift.

00:12:09.520 --> 00:12:11.270
Rebecca can recall happy memories

00:12:11.270 --> 00:12:13.550
in moments when times get tough.

00:12:13.550 --> 00:12:16.760
- [Rebecca] A good memory
that makes me so thankful

00:12:16.760 --> 00:12:20.223
about having this kind of H.S.A.M. memory.

00:12:22.470 --> 00:12:23.830
- [Narrator] Thanks for chatting, Rebecca.

00:12:23.830 --> 00:12:26.000
- [Rebecca] Yup, thanks, bye.

00:12:26.000 --> 00:12:28.583
(light music)

